On Mar 27, 1:39 pm, Ohle Claussen <claus...@dpi.physik.uni-
goettingen.de> wrote:
> On 2007-03-27, krono...@avengingangel.us wrote:
> > A lot of people have pointed out that there are quite a few artifacts,
> > especially artifact weapons, that suck, and a few more that are rather
> > mediocre. I've got here a list of ideas on how to fix this, to a
> > point.
>
> > All 'foobanes' (those that affect only a very narrow group of foes) -
> > +d24 damage instead of double damage. +d5 to hit versus ALL foes, add
> > an additional +5 to hit versus chosen foe. This means the extra damage
> > is independent of enchantment and makes these weapons a bit more
> > useful, maybe worth keeping around just in case.
>
> But as long as they're still only effective against one subclass, it's
> still easier to just ignore them.
>

> > Werebane: Cures lycanthropy when #invoked, lycanthropes flee from it
> > if it's on the ground (as if it were Elbereth).
>
> Hardly notable effects.

True. But, Werebane is already one of the more useful foobanes (mostly
by dint of being a silver saber, but still)

> > Ogresmasher: Adds enchantment to the wielder's CON.
>
> You'd still have to wield a worthless weapon, which outweighs the benefits.

Frankly, I couldn't come up with a way to make Ogresmasher actually
*not* suck without making it Mojo mk. 2.

> > Sting: Warning versus all monsters.
>
> That would be incredibly useful. But Sting already is a passable early game
> artifact, since it's an elven dagger, which is a good practicing weapon for
> classes that can use daggers, and because orcs are rather common in the
> early game. Maybe it doesn't need improvement at all.

Possibly, but it still increments the artifact count. If it provided
warning, that might be incentive enough to actually make it.

> > Orcrist: May behead orcs (10% chance), confers poison resistance while
> > wielded.
>
> The PR is nice.
>
> > Snickersnee: +d5 to hit (on top of the innate +1 to hit for being a
> > katana), +1d14 damage. IMHO, since Snickersnee is a bit harder to get
> > than Excalibur, and has none of EX's special effects, it should be a
> > more powerful weapon.
>
> Well, something distinguishing the two might be in order, yes. Maybe a
> special effect rather than higher numbers?

I was sorta struggling to come up with one, but the conclusion I came
to is that EX does shiny stuff, while Snickersnee should be just a
whacking-iron. But if that's the case, it should whack *better* than
Excalibur, which both does shiny stuff and is generally easier to get.

> > Demonbane: Cannot be bullwhipped away, 50% chance to 'bite deeply' and
> > deal an extra +1d30 damage to demons.
>
> Useless early and mid game; in Gehennom you'll be slaying demons left and
> right anyway.

Fair point, though with +1d24+1d30 damage it actually outdamages
Grayswandir, at least sometimes. This would probably make Demonbane a
worthwhile choice in Gehennom, unlike now where it's only useful there
if you've really had an unlucky run of things and are still mucking by
with a long sword, say. Even a non-artifact silver saber is generally
better.

> > Stormbringer: +1d2 bonus replaced with flat +2.
>
> Why? It's quite a good weapon as it is.

Ok, I rescind this one and Grimtooth. Was pretty arbitrary anyway.

> > Cleaver: +d8 instead of +d6 damage, +d5 instead of +d3 to hit.
>
> Wouldn't make it much more popular. It's still a battle axe, so only useful
> to certain classes, and it's already a good early to mid game choice for
> those.
>
> > Tsurugi of Muramasa: Double damage instead of +d8
>
> The Tsurugi already is a killer weapon. Its only downside is the
> two-handedness.

I was thinking that the damage upgrade might make the two-handedness
worth it. As it stands, although the Tsurugi is pretty solid, dual-
wielding EX, SN or GS (or maybe FB) with a katana or silver saber is
generally a better choice for a samurai.

> > Vorpal Blade: +d5 to hit, +d8 damage instead of +1.
>
> I'd like some damage and to-hit increase on vorpy, yes. Not sure wether
> these numbers are appropriate.
>
> > Grimtooth: bonus damage +d8 instead of +d6
>
> I don't quite see the reason for this.
